Manila, the Philippines. It was designed by William E. Parsons. The Manila Elks Lodge #761 is a unique branch club of the Benevolent and Protective Order of Elks—BPOE, an American fraternal order. It is one of only two Elks Club established outside of current United States territory, the other being in Panama near the former American territory of the Panama Canal Zone, The Manila Elks Club was founded after the\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0118-17e5c5bdb7a3:d01\", \"text\": \"Town or city Manila Country Philippines Coordinates 14°34′45″N 120°58′38″E ﻿ / ﻿ 14.57907°N 120.97711°E ﻿ / 14.57907 ; 120.97711 Current tenants Museo Pambata Named for Elks Club Completed 1949 Technical details Floor count 2 Design and construction Architect ( s ) William E. Parsons The Elks Club Building is the former clubhouse of the Manila Elks Lodge # 761—Manila Lodge 761 , also known as the Manila Elks Club , located in Manila , Philippines . [ 1 ] It was designed by William E. Parsons . The Manila Elks Lodge # 761 is a branch club of the Benevolent\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0118-17e5c5bdb7a3:d02\", \"text\": \"known as the Manila Elks Club, in Manila, the Philippines. It was designed by Frank Lloyd Wright. The Manila Elks Lodge # 761 is a unique branch club of the Benevolent and Protective Order of Elks—BPOE, an American fraternal order. It is one of only two Elks Club established outside of current United States territory, the other being in Panama near the former American territory of the Panama Canal Zone, (en) dbo:thumbnail wiki-commons:Special:FilePath/WTMP_Team_Kampai_DSC_0023.jpg?width=300 dbo:wikiPageExternalLink http://www.manilaelks.org dbo:wikiPageID\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0118-17e5c5bdb7a3:d03\", \"text\": \"Elks Club Building (Tyler, Texas) The Elks Club Building in Tyler, Texas is an International Style building built in 1949. It was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 2002. It is a three-story, two-part commercial block designed by Tyler architect Carl A. Gregory (1903-1976) and constructed by Tyler contractor Hugh E. White. The local Benevolent and Protective Order of Elks club used its second and third floors for offices, dining room and ballroom, and let out the first-floor retail space, until the club sold the building in 1973 and moved elsewhere. Its NRHP nomination describes its International\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0118-17e5c5bdb7a3:d04\", \"text\": \"Register of Historic Places in 2002 . [ 1 ] History [ edit ] It is a three-story , two-part commercial block designed by Tyler architect Carl A. Gregory ( 1903–1976 ) and constructed by Tyler contractor Hugh E. White . The local Benevolent and Protective Order of Elks club used its second and third floors for offices , dining room and ballroom , and let out the first-floor retail space , until the club sold the building in 1973 and moved elsewhere . [ 2 ] Its NRHP nomination describes its International Style elements as including its volumetric massing\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0118-17e5c5bdb7a3:d05\", \"text\": \", 2025 Christine Torres Manila News-Intelligencer Newspaper 56 1103 Sgt . Esguerra Ave Diliman , Quezon City Metro Manila , Philippines +6 ( 328 ) 835-4500 Disclaimer Welcome to our Manila satire site , where reality takes a backseat ! Our content is as satirical as traffic is congested in EDSA , so don ’ t take it too seriously . Find our disclaimer in every nook , from headers to footers , ensuring you ’ re in on the joke . Curious about our jesting journey ? Our ‘ About Us ’ page spills the beans on our satirical\"}], \"fallible_prior\": [], \"prior_explanation\": \"I am uncertain about the specific architect of 'the' Elks Club Building, as many exist globally with different designers. Without a specified location, I cannot provide a definitive answer.\", \"fallible_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0118-17e5c5bdb7a3:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0118-17e5c5bdb7a3:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"William E. Parsons\", \"scope\": \"Architect of the Elks Club Building (Manila)\", \"claim\": \"The Elks Club Building in Manila was designed by William E. Parsons.\", \"answer\": \"William E. Parsons\", \"quote\": \"It was designed by William E. Parsons.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0118-17e5c5bdb7a3:d01\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0118-17e5c5bdb7a3:d01:c0\", \"entity\": \"William E. Parsons\", \"scope\": \"Architect of the Elks Club Building\", \"claim\": \"The Elks Club Building was designed by William E. Parsons.\", \"answer\": \"William E. Parsons\", \"quote\": \"Town or city Manila Country Philippines Coordinates 14°34′45″N 120°58′38″E ﻿ / ﻿ 14.57907°N 120.97711°E ﻿ / 14.57907 ; 120.97711 Current tenants Museo Pambata Named for Elks Club Completed 1949 Technical details Floor count 2 Design and construction Architect ( s ) William E. Parsons The Elks Club Building is the former clubhouse of the Manila Elks Lodge # 761—Manila Lodge 761 , also known as the Manila Elks Club , located in Manila , Philippines . [ 1 ] It was designed by William E. Parsons .\", \"original_document_number\": 2}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0118-17e5c5bdb7a3:d02\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0118-17e5c5bdb7a3:d02:c0\", \"entity\": \"Frank Lloyd Wright\", \"scope\": \"Architect of the Elks Club Building\", \"claim\": \"The Elks Club Building was designed by Frank Lloyd Wright.\", \"answer\": \"Frank Lloyd Wright\", \"quote\": \"It was designed by Frank Lloyd Wright.\", \"original_document_number\": 3}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0118-17e5c5bdb7a3:d03\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0118-17e5c5bdb7a3:d03:c0\", \"entity\": \"Carl A. Gregory\", \"scope\": \"Architect of the Elks Club Building in Tyler, Texas\", \"claim\": \"The Elks Club Building in Tyler, Texas was designed by Tyler architect Carl A. Gregory.\", \"answer\": \"Carl A. Gregory\", \"quote\": \"It is a three-story, two-part commercial block designed by Tyler architect Carl A. Gregory (1903-1976) and constructed by Tyler contractor Hugh E.\", \"original_document_number\":